- [x] cockpit-server on a VPS: curl installer (Linux x86_64 + arm64), release tarballs, docs, validation <!-- id: k25 labels: feature, docs -->
      Why: the desktop client already installs the server over SSH from its embedded bundle, but the mobile client (iPad/Android) carries no bundle and can only connect to a host a desktop prepared first. A VPS needs a way to get the server without a desktop.
      Decisions (2026-09-15): install dir stays `~/.cockpit/server/{bin,lib}` + `bundle.manifest` (what the client looks for; no sudo, no systemd, the client starts the server on demand over SSH with --exit-on-idle); client and server must be the same release (mobile already reports version_mismatch); Linux only, x86_64 and arm64.
      Steps:
      1. CI: the linux jobs already build the native server bundle for amd64/arm64. Pack `cockpit-server-<version>-linux-x86_64.tar.gz` and `-linux-arm64.tar.gz` (bin/ + lib/ + bundle.manifest), attach to the GitHub Release, list in SHA256SUMS and latest.json.
      2. Script `cockpit/install-server.sh` (site URL redirects to the GitHub raw) (same style as install.sh): `curl -fsSL https://remote-pi.jacobmoura.work/cockpit-server.sh | bash`. Detect `uname -m` (x86_64 | aarch64), resolve version (latest via latest.json, or `COCKPIT_VERSION=x.y.z`), download tarball + SHA256SUMS, verify, stage, atomic swap into `~/.cockpit/server` (same no-nest rename the client uses), smoke run `cockpit-server --socket <tmp> --exit-on-idle 1`, print next step (connect from Cockpit). Idempotent: same version installed = no-op. Re-run = update.
      3. Docs on the site: "Remote host on a VPS" page: requirements (SSH key, Linux x86_64/arm64, glibc note), the curl one-liner, how updates work (desktop auto-updates over SSH, mobile needs the curl again), troubleshooting (version_mismatch, missing libcockpit_pty.so, socket permissions).
      5. Service (decided 2026-09-15): subcommand `cockpit-server service install|uninstall|status` writes `~/.config/systemd/user/cockpit-server.service` (ExecStart without --exit-on-idle, Restart=on-failure, COCKPIT_PTY_DYLIB env), `systemctl --user enable --now`, tries `loginctl enable-linger` and prints the sudo command if denied. install.sh and the desktop SSH update restart the unit after swapping the bundle. `cockpit-server.sh --service` opts in; default stays on-demand. Linux only (launchd later if needed).
      Packaging (decided): one zip per arch, `cockpit-server-<version>-linux-{x86_64,arm64}.zip` containing `cockpit-server/{bin,lib,bundle.manifest,VERSION,install.sh}`; separate tag `cockpit-server-v<version>` and workflow (app release keeps apk/aab). install.sh is arch-agnostic (checks uname -m against VERSION, sha256sum -c, atomic swap, smoke run, restore backup on failure); the site script only resolves version/arch, downloads, verifies and calls install.sh.
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-15T18:00 -->
      Steps 1, 2, 3 and 5 implemented: `tool/build-server-zip.sh` + `packages/cockpit_server/install.sh` (manifest, VERSION, smoke, atomic swap, --service), `cockpit-server service install|uninstall|status` and `--version` in the binary (tests in service_manager_test.dart, Linux-only cases run in CI), workflow `.github/workflows/cockpit-server-release.yml` on tag `cockpit-server-v<version>` (tests, build, installer smoke on the runner, release with SHA256SUMS), `cockpit/install-server.sh` (site URL redirects to the GitHub raw), docs section "Remote hosts & VPS" on the site. Open: first tag to validate the workflow, then step 4 on real VPS.
      4. Validate on two real VPS (one x86_64, one arm64), including an older distro (Ubuntu 22.04 / Debian 12) to catch the glibc floor of the ubuntu-24.04 build; if it fails, build the x86_64 slice with the Zig cross-link targeting glibc 2.31 like the arm64 slice. Test from the desktop and from the iPad. Feed findings into k13 and the docs.
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-16T00:30 -->
      Done. Release cockpit-server-v1.28.32 published (x86_64 + arm64 zips, re-generated after three fixes: arm64 Flutter via git, stdout flush and first-line VERSION in --version, PTY lib resolved from bin/../lib, PATH link on every run, unzip fallback). Validated on an OrbStack Ubuntu arm64: curl installer, `cockpit-server --version`, remote workspace opened from the desktop (host alias `ubuntu@orb`, client reused the curl install without reinstalling), commands round-trip. Service kept as optional (`--service`): value today is start at boot, never idle-exit and systemd restart; the client still bootstraps its own server if it finds none, so a killed server "comes back" either way. Follow-ups (not blocking): client should respect an existing systemd unit instead of bootstrapping; session restore after host reboot; old-glibc VPS check still pending.

