- [x] Files pane: right-click on the empty area below the tree does nothing, so you cannot paste into the root <!-- id: k20 labels: bug -->
      The context menu only opens on a file or folder row. Clicking the empty space at the bottom of the pane (or inside an empty folder) shows nothing, so there is no way to paste, create a file or folder at the workspace root without picking a folder first. The empty area should open the folder menu targeting the root (or the folder being viewed when it is empty).
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-15T14:00 -->
      Root cause: the folder context menu lived only in _RowState._showMenu, so nothing below the last row (or inside an empty expanded folder) was hit-testable. Fix: extracted the menu into _showNodeMenu(context, pos, spec) reused as-is; the tree background (ContextMenuGesture translucent around the scroll view) opens it targeting the workspace root, and an empty expanded folder renders an invisible 26px slot that opens it targeting that folder. Root spec hides rename/delete/copy/cut (no callback). Right-click on desktop, long-press on touch.

- [x] Task debug tab opens empty (no output) <!-- id: k26 labels: bug -->
      Running a task from the Tasks panel opens the task output tab but it stays blank: no command echo, no stdout/stderr, no exit status. Check whether the runner actually spawned (PtyTaskRunner, `launchctl asuser` prefix on macOS, login shell `-ilc`), whether the TaskOutputSession is attached to the right tab (see k23: `openTaskOutput` writes `active` into the focused pane), and whether output is gated by the visible/active flags of the pane. May share the root cause with k23.
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-15T11:00 -->
      k23 fixed (task tab now activates in its own pane; pane_view compares with the shown index). Re-test this one on the next build: if the blank tab was the focused pane losing its active tab, it is the same bug. If the task tab itself still shows nothing, it is the runner/output path.
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-15T14:00 -->
      Root cause in TaskTerminalStore._onRun: the guard `_lastPid[id] == run.pid` read an absent key as null, so a running run without pid never subscribed, and `_lastPid` was never cleared at exit, so a re-run that got the same pid back from the OS kept the subscription on the closed stream of the previous process (blank tab). Fix: containsKey guard + `_lastPid.remove` when the run ends. Runner ordering (PtyTaskRunner/RemoteTaskRunner set `_running` before `_emit(running)`, sync broadcast) was verified correct and is now pinned by test/session/task_terminal_store_run_ordering_test.dart. The UI-side blank (pane active index) was k23.

- [x] Opening a .ckp layout should replace the current layout (close the existing tabs first) <!-- id: k21 labels: feature -->
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-15T14:00 -->
      Done. Opening a layout now means "become this layout": `applyLayoutFile` defaults to `LayoutApplyMode.replace`, which validates the .ckp first (a parse error closes nothing), then closes every tab of the workspace (labelled/pinned tabs included, PTYs and agents disposed through the same path as the tab "x") and only then builds the panes, so the split geometry is deterministic. The Files pane "Open layout" asks "Replace the current layout? N tabs will be closed" only when a tab has running work (agent mid-turn, harness running in a terminal, task with a live process); idle tabs close without a prompt. `cockpit orchestrate` replaces without asking and keeps the tab it is run from (closing it would kill the CLI mid-call); the new `--append` flag restores the old idempotent merge, and the output/JSON gained `closed: N`. The worktree autorun keeps appending (fresh workspace, no UI). Ordering lives in `domain/services/layout_apply_runner.dart` with tests in `test/layout/layout_apply_runner_test.dart` (replace closes, append keeps, invalid file leaves tabs untouched).

      Today applying a .ckp file adds its terminals and splits on top of whatever is already open, so the workspace ends up with the old tabs plus the new ones. Opening a layout should mean "become this layout": close the current tabs of the workspace, then build the panes described in the file. Applies to opening from the Files pane and to `cockpit orchestrate`. Consider a confirmation when a tab has a running process.

- [x] Code/text editor: Tab inserts indentation instead of moving focus <!-- id: k22 labels: feature -->
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-15T14:00 -->
      Done in `code_editor.dart`: the TextField is wrapped in `Actions` overriding `NextFocusIntent`/`PreviousFocusIntent`, so Tab and Shift+Tab never leave the editor. Tab with a caret inserts spaces up to the next tab stop (a real tab when the file already uses tabs); Shift+Tab removes up to one indent unit from the line start; with a multi-line selection both act on every selected line and the selection keeps covering the same lines. Indent unit is detected from the content (tabs, else the smallest indentation delta among the first 200 indented lines) with a fallback by extension (2 for dart/yaml/json/js/ts/html/css/md, 4 otherwise). Pure logic lives in `editor_indent.dart` and is applied through `controller.value`, so undo keeps working. Tests: `test/ui/editor_indent_test.dart` and `test/ui/code_editor_tab_indent_test.dart`. Escape-then-Tab focus escape was skipped.

      The file editor (`code_editor.dart`) is a plain TextField, so Tab follows Flutter's default and moves keyboard focus out of the editor. Make it behave like VS Code: Tab inserts spaces up to the next tab stop (or a real tab when the file already uses tabs), Shift+Tab outdents. With a multi-line selection, Tab/Shift+Tab indent/outdent every selected line. Implementation: wrap the editor in `Actions` overriding `NextFocusIntent`/`PreviousFocusIntent` (or a `Focus.onKeyEvent` that handles Tab and returns handled) and edit the controller text/selection directly, keeping undo. Indent unit: detect from the file content (tabs vs spaces, width from the first indented lines); fallback 2 for Dart/YAML/JSON, 4 elsewhere. Escape then Tab could restore focus traversal for accessibility, as VS Code does.

- [x] Windows/Linux: "Open with Cockpit" (file association + single instance) <!-- id: k17 labels: feature -->
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-13T16:40 -->
      Part 3 done (plugins + present channel in windows/runner/flutter_window.cpp and linux/runner/my_application.cc), so "Open in new window" from inside the app now gets plugins, title and size on Windows/Linux too. Written without a local toolchain: the first CI build for those targets validates it. Still open: single instance (forward the path to the running app via the CLI socket) and file association (Inno Setup / .desktop MimeType).
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-15T10:00 -->
      1.28.32 validated by hand on Windows and Linux: "Open in new window" works (plugins, title, size). Only single instance and file association remain.

      macOS is done (k10). On Windows/Linux the OS starts a NEW process with the file path as argument: detect a running Cockpit (the CLI socket already exists), forward the path to it and exit; otherwise open a document window directly. Association: Inno Setup registry keys (Windows) and MimeType in the .desktop (Linux). Also register the desktop_multi_window plugin callback on those platforms (windows/runner + linux/my_application.cc) so document windows get plugins.
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-15T12:30 -->
      Single instance + association done (blind, CI compiles): second process forwards `open-document` over the CLI socket (POSIX UDS; Windows reads `~/.cockpit/status.json` with port+token that the app now writes) and exits, else boots and opens the files after bind. Inno Setup registers Cockpit.Kanban/Layout/Query/Http ProgIds (default for .kanban/.ckp/.dbq/.http, "Open with" only for .md). Linux: `Exec=cockpit %F`, MimeType list, shared-mime XML installed to share/mime/packages. To verify on a real machine: double-click a .kanban with Cockpit closed and open; `cockpit` CLI outside a pane on Windows.

- [x] Running a task blanks the terminal in the other pane when a task tab already exists <!-- id: k23 labels: bug -->
      Repro: two panes side by side; the task (debug) tab lives in the right pane and you are working in a terminal on the left pane. Run the task again. The task tab is reused correctly, but the left pane goes blank (the terminal text disappears). Clicking the left tab again brings it back. Looks like focusing/re-selecting the existing task tab in the right pane re-renders or detaches the terminal view of the pane that was active, so it may be a general pane bug: any cross-pane tab activation (task run, `cockpit` CLI focus, click on a notification) could blank the previously active pane. Check the pane/tab selection path and the terminal view keep-alive when the active pane changes without user input.
      <!-- comment: 2026-09-14T09:30 -->
      Root cause found: `openTaskOutput` (cockpit_viewmodel.dart ~1036-1047) writes `active = <task tab id>` into the FOCUSED pane instead of the pane that owns the task tab. The focused pane then has an orphan `active`, `pane_view.dart` marks every tab of it inactive, and `terminal_pane.dart:535` returns an empty box for inactive terminals (the buffer is intact, which is why clicking restores it). Only this path has the defect: browser/DB reuse and goBackInPane already look up the owning leaf. CLI `run-task` does not call `openTaskOutput`, so the trigger is the Tasks panel. Fix: find the leaf containing the session and call `selectTab(leaf.id, tabId)`. Hardening: in pane_view compare against `tabs[activeIndex]` instead of `pane.active` so a transient invalid `active` never blanks a whole pane.

- [x] Kanban: long-press (or click-and-hold) on the advance arrow sends the card straight to the last column <!-- id: k19 labels: feature -->
      Today the arrow moves the card one column forward. A long press or touch-and-hold on the same button should skip the intermediate columns and drop the card in the last column (Done). A short click keeps the current one-step behavior. Blocked cards still have no button.

- [x] Decide: macOS sandbox stays off? <!-- labels: decision -->
      Decided 2026-09-12: sandbox stays OFF on desktop, by design. Cockpit spawns shells, git, ssh, pi and LSP servers, reads any project folder and owns a Unix socket; none of that fits the App Sandbox. Sandbox is only required for the Mac App Store, which is out of scope. Hardened Runtime, notarization and TCC prompts stay. Mobile clients (iOS/Android) are sandboxed by the platform and do not run local shells. Follow-up: review whether cs.disable-library-validation is still needed in Release.entitlements.

- [x] Keyboard shortcut to switch between workspaces <!-- labels: feature -->
      Done 2026-09-12: Cmd+Shift+N (previous) and Cmd+Shift+M (next), Ctrl+Shift on Windows/Linux, in the View menu next to Select Tab. Cycles the rail in visual order (root then its worktrees), wraps around, skips the Cockpit system terminal. No digit shortcuts on purpose: worktrees make positions unstable.

- [x] Explicit approval for .env.cockpit in a freshly cloned repo <!-- labels: security -->
      Done 2026-09-12, downscoped: no trust dialog (a cloned repo already runs its own scripts and hooks). Instead: (1) keys that change who runs what are never injected (PATH, SHELL, HOME, ZDOTDIR, BASH_ENV, ENV, PROMPT_COMMAND, IFS, LD_* and DYLD_*); (2) when the .env.cockpit is tracked by git (it came with the repository) new terminals print a yellow notice listing the injected key names, never values.

- [x] Drag-and-drop a file into the terminal steals the tab focus <!-- labels: bug -->
      Fixed 2026-09-12: a native drop generates no pointer event and the drag from Finder/Explorer leaves the Cockpit window non-key, so no focus path ran. After injecting the paths the terminal now brings the window to front, focuses its pane in the VM and re-requests the terminal focus node post-frame. Needs a manual check with a real drop after the next release.

